    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0834-6834'
abstract: >-
  Replication package for a study testing whether the disagreement between a
  large language model and a human annotator is distinguishable from the
  disagreement between two human annotators. Two annotators independently
  coded 160 Spanish-language thesis abstracts from four institutional
  repositories in Puno, Peru, on a 12-variable protocol covering IMRaD
  adherence, organisational coherence, component completeness and
  methodological consistency. Three open-weight models (gpt-oss-20B,
  Mistral 7B, Qwen3 8B) coded the same abstracts from the verbatim human
  codebook, three runs each. The package contains the full analysis
  pipeline, the annotations of all annotators and models, and the derived
  agreement statistics, figures and error analysis.

  Abstract texts and thesis titles are not redistributed: they were
  harvested from public institutional repositories and their copyright
  belongs to the respective authors and institutions. The persistent handle
  of every record is included so that the exact text can be retrieved from
  the source repository and every analysis reproduced.
keywords:
  - inter-annotator agreement
  - large language models
  - abstract quality
  - institutional repositories
  - Cohen's kappa
  - annotation reliability
